# Civic App — Mandatory Rules

This file is the persistent, always-loaded ruleset for this project. It exists
because `API_SOURCES.md` and `OBJECTIVE_SOURCES.md` contain the detailed
reference material (endpoints, keys, per-source lean labels) but were only
ever applied when manually pasted into a chat. That meant every fresh session
"forgot" the rules, which is why past data collection produced inconsistent,
partial `Politician` / `Election` / `CountyOfficial` records. Do not repeat
that pattern — treat every rule below as mandatory, not advisory, on every
task in this repo, without needing to be reminded.

Full endpoint/key reference: `API_SOURCES.md`.
Full source-lean/tier reference: `OBJECTIVE_SOURCES.md`.
Next.js version note: `AGENTS.md` (read `node_modules/next/dist/docs/` before
using any App Router / data-fetching API — this project's Next.js has
breaking changes vs. training data).

## 1. Architecture — non-negotiable

- Never call an external API (ProPublica, FEC, NewsAPI, etc.) directly from
  a client component or the browser. Every external call is proxied through
  a route in `app/api/`.
- Every external client lives in `lib/api/<service>.ts` and returns a typed
  response using the shared types in `lib/types/index.ts` (extend that file
  rather than inventing ad-hoc shapes).
- Cache every external API response with a TTL. If Supabase is configured,
  cache there using the `sources` table shape defined in
  `OBJECTIVE_SOURCES.md`; otherwise cache in-memory/on-disk with an explicit
  TTL constant — never re-fetch on every page load.
- Every external fetch gets exponential backoff on failure: 2s, 4s, 8s, 16s.
  After the final retry, fail into the missing-data protocol in §3 — never
  throw an unhandled error that blanks a whole page.
- Log every cache hit/miss so data freshness is debuggable.

## 2. Source-of-truth priority order

When two sources disagree on the same fact, prefer the higher-ranked source
and cite it; do not average or silently pick one:

1. Official .gov records (Congress.gov, FEC, Senate.gov, Federal Register)
2. ProPublica
3. AP
4. Reuters
5. GovTrack
6. Voteview
7. Ballotpedia
8. OpenSecrets
9. Pew Research
10. VoteSmart
11. PolitiFact
12. FactCheck.org
13. CBO

## 3. Data completeness contract (fixes incomplete profiles)

A `Politician`, `Election`, `Candidate`, or `CountyOfficial` record is
**not done** just because the page renders without a crash. Before treating
any profile as complete:

- Every **required** (non-`?`) field on the type in `lib/types/index.ts`
  must be populated with real, sourced data — not `''`, `0`, `[]`, or a
  placeholder string standing in for "unknown."
- If a required field's real value genuinely isn't available yet from any
  listed source, do not leave it blank or fabricate a value. Instead:
  1. Populate it with the best sourced value you can find and cite, or
  2. If nothing is available, add/update a `dataNote` (on `Election`) or an
     equivalent inline note explaining what's missing and why, so the gap is
     visible rather than silently rendered as empty.
- Optional fields (`?`) that a page section depends on (e.g. `endorsements`,
  `statementsVsActions`, `approvalRating`) should still be filled whenever a
  source exists — don't skip them just because they're optional in the type.
- Every array of records (`votingRecord`, `campaignFinance.donors`,
  `stockTrades`, `controversies`, `news`, `topIssues`, `statementsVsActions`,
  `consistency.campaignPromises`) must have every item carry a `source`
  (or `evidenceSource`) per §4 — an item with no source is incomplete, not
  just unsourced.
- When adding a new page/section that reads politician or election data, add
  the fields it needs to the shared types first, then populate them for
  every existing record — never let one page's needs create a field that's
  only filled in for some profiles.

## 4. Sourcing & objectivity rules

- Every `Source` object must set `tier: SourceTier` (`official` |
  `nonpartisan` | `media` | `alleged` | `unverified`) per the definitions in
  `lib/types/index.ts` and the tables in `OBJECTIVE_SOURCES.md`. Never leave
  a source untiered.
- Any source with a documented political lean (see the lean table in
  `OBJECTIVE_SOURCES.md`) must have that lean surfaced in the UI wherever
  its content is shown. Never display lean-source content without its lean
  label — labeling multiple perspectives is the project's core credibility
  mechanism, not an optional annotation.
- `NewsItem.isOpinion` must be `true` for any op-ed/editorial/column,
  regardless of outlet, and opinion content is never used as the sole source
  for a factual claim — only for "public reaction" style sections.
- `Controversy.isVerified` may only be `true` when backed by an `official`
  or `nonpartisan` tier source; otherwise it stays `false` and the item is
  tiered `alleged` or `unverified`.
- `StatementVsAction` requires both a sourced `statement` and a sourced
  `action` — never create one half without the other.
- `CampaignPromise.status` other than `'In Progress'` requires `evidence`
  and `evidenceSource` — a verdict without evidence is not allowed.
- Wire services (AP, Reuters) and official records are the default choice
  for factual claims; use editorial/leaning outlets only for
  perspective/context sections, always labeled.

## 5. Quick checklist before calling a profile/page "done"

- [ ] All required fields populated with real, sourced values (§3)
- [ ] Every array item has a tiered `Source` (§4)
- [ ] Any genuinely unavailable field has a `dataNote` / explicit gap marker
      instead of being blank
- [ ] Lean labels shown for any leaning source used
- [ ] No direct external API calls from client code (§1)
- [ ] New/changed fields added to `lib/types/index.ts`, not ad-hoc shapes
